```
PATCH /api/users/123
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Jane Doe"
}
```
- **Unsafe**: Modifies resource
- **May or may not be idempotent**: Depends on implementation
- **Partial update**: Only specified fields are updated
- **RFC 6902**: Defines standard PATCH operations

### 🔑 PATCH - KEY POINTS & NOTES
```
✓ Use for partial updates - only send changed fields
✓ More bandwidth efficient than PUT
✓ Return 200 OK with updated resource
✓ Non-idempotent by default (carefully design if idempotency needed)
⚠️ RFC 6902 defines standard JSON Patch operations (add, remove, replace, move, copy, test)
⚠️ Different from simple merge - specify operation types for clarity
⚠️ Document carefully - different servers may handle PATCH differently
```

### 💡 PRACTICAL EXAMPLES
```javascript
// Node.js/Express - Simple merge PATCH
app.patch('/api/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;
  const updates = req.body; // Only changed fields

  try {
    const user = await User.findById(id);
    if (!user) {
      return res.status(404).json({
        error: { code: 'NOT_FOUND', message: 'User not found' }
      });
    }

    // Validate email if being updated
    if (updates.email && updates.email !== user.email) {
      const existing = await User.findOne({ email: updates.email });
      if (existing) {
        return res.status(409).json({
          error: { code: 'DUPLICATE_EMAIL', message: 'Email already exists' }
        });
      }
    }

    // Merge updates
    const updated = await User.findByIdAndUpdate(id, updates, {
      new: true,
      runValidators: true
    });

    res.status(200).json({
      success: true,
      data: updated
    });
  } catch (error) {
    res.status(500).json({
      error: { code: 'SERVER_ERROR', message: error.message }
    });
  }
});

// Angular - Simple merge
updateUserPartial(id: string, updates: Partial<User>) {
  return this.http.patch<User>(`/api/users/${id}`, updates);
}

// React - Simple merge PATCH
const updateUserPartial = async (userId, updates) => {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, {
    method: 'PATCH',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(updates)  // Only { name: "Jane" }
  });

  return response.json();
};
```

