## REST Principles & Constraints

### 1. Client-Server Separation
- Client and server are independent
- Communication only through HTTP
- Allows independent evolution

### 2. Statelessness
- No client context stored on server
- Each request is complete and independent
- Improves reliability and scalability

```javascript
// ✗ Bad: Stateful
let currentUser;
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  currentUser = req.body.user; // Stores state
});

// ✓ Good: Stateless
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const token = generateToken(req.body.user);
  res.json({ token }); // Each request includes token
});
```

### 3. Cacheability
- Responses should be cacheable
- Reduces network traffic
- Improves performance

```
Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600    # Cache for 1 hour
Cache-Control: private, max-age=1800   # Cache for 30 min
Cache-Control: no-cache                # Always validate
Cache-Control: no-store                # Never cache
```

### 4. Uniform Interface
- Consistent way to interact with resources
- Standard HTTP methods
- Predictable URI structure

### 5. Layered System
- Client doesn't know if connected directly to end server
- Allows intermediaries (proxies, caches, load balancers)
- Improves scalability

### 6. Code on Demand (Optional)
- Server can extend client functionality
- Applets, scripts
- Less commonly used

