## Versioning

### Why Version APIs?
- **Breaking changes**: Need to maintain backward compatibility
- **Features**: New features shouldn't break existing clients
- **Deprecation**: Gradual phase-out of old endpoints

### Versioning Strategies

#### 1. URI Path Versioning (Most Common)
```
GET /api/v1/users
GET /api/v2/users

# Advantages: Clear, easy to test, easy to route
# Disadvantages: Different base URLs
```

#### 2. Header Versioning
```
GET /api/users
Headers: API-Version: 1

# Advantages: Same base URL
# Disadvantages: Less visible, harder to test
```

#### 3. Accept Header
```
GET /api/users
Headers: Accept: application/vnd.api.v1+json

# Advantages: Content negotiation standard
# Disadvantages: Complex, less intuitive
```

#### 4. Query Parameter
```
GET /api/users?version=1

# Advantages: Easy to test
# Disadvantages: Messy, not RESTful
```

### Versioning Strategy Example
```
v1 - Initial release
v1.1 - Bug fixes, new optional fields (backward compatible)
v2 - Breaking changes (required for incompatible updates)
v3 - Another breaking change

# API Lifecycle
Stable (v1, v2)
  ↓
Deprecated (announce 6-12 months before sunset)
  ↓
Sunset (6 months notice before removal)
  ↓
Removed
```

### Deprecation Headers
```
Headers:
  Deprecation: true
  Sunset: Sun, 29 Dec 2025 23:59:59 GMT
  Link: </api/v2/users>; rel="successor-version"

Response Body:
{
  "success": true,
  "data": { ... },
  "deprecation": {
    "message": "This endpoint will be removed on 2025-12-29",
    "newEndpoint": "/api/v2/users",
    "suggestionLink": "https://docs.example.com/migration"
  }
}
```

