### 8. HTTP METHODS - THEORETICAL ASPECTS

**GET Method**:
- **Safe**: Doesn't modify data
- **Idempotent**: Same request produces same result
- **Cacheable**: Results can be cached
- **No request body**: Data in query parameters
- **Use case**: Retrieve resources

**POST Method**:
- **Unsafe**: Can modify data
- **Non-idempotent**: Multiple requests create multiple resources
- **Has request body**: Data in message body
- **Not usually cached**: Dynamic operations
- **Use case**: Create new resources or perform actions

**PUT Method**:
- **Unsafe**: Modifies resource
- **Idempotent**: Multiple identical requests have same effect
- **Full replacement**: Send complete resource representation
- **Has request body**: Complete resource data
- **Use case**: Replace entire resource

**PATCH Method**:
- **Unsafe**: Modifies resource
- **May or may not be idempotent**: Depends on implementation
- **Partial update**: Only changed fields
- **Has request body**: Partial resource data
- **Use case**: Update specific fields

**DELETE Method**:
- **Unsafe**: Removes resource
- **Idempotent**: Deleting again has same effect
- **Usually no body**: Just identifier needed
- **Use case**: Remove resource

**HEAD Method**:
- **Safe**: Like GET but no body
- **Idempotent**: Same as GET
- **Use case**: Check resource existence, get headers

**OPTIONS Method**:
- **Safe**: Lists available methods
- **Idempotent**: Always returns same options
- **Use case**: CORS preflight, discover capabilities

