### 23. RATE LIMITING - THEORETICAL CONCEPTS

**Purpose**: Control request rate to prevent abuse and overload.

**Why Rate Limit**:
- **Prevent abuse**: Stop malicious actors
- **Prevent overload**: Protect server
- **Fair usage**: Ensure fair distribution
- **Cost control**: Limit API usage costs
- **DoS protection**: Mitigate attacks

**Rate Limiting Algorithms**:

**Token Bucket**:
- Tokens replenished at fixed rate
- Each request costs 1 token
- Allows burst traffic
- Smooths out traffic

**Sliding Window**:
- Track requests in time window
- Reject if limit exceeded
- Prevents burst gaming
- Memory intensive

**Fixed Window**:
- Fixed time periods
- Reset counter per period
- Simple to implement
- Allows burst at boundaries

**Leaky Bucket**:
- Fixed outflow rate
- Requests queued
- Smooths traffic
- Can have lag

**Rate Limit Dimensions**:
- **Per IP**: Rate limit by client IP
- **Per User**: Rate limit by authenticated user
- **Per API Key**: Rate limit by API key
- **Global**: Single limit for all

**Rate Limit Tiers**:
- **Free**: Lower limits
- **Premium**: Higher limits
- **Enterprise**: Custom limits or unlimited

**Rate Limit Communication**:
- **Headers**: X-RateLimit-Limit, X-RateLimit-Remaining, X-RateLimit-Reset
- **Status code**: 429 Too Many Requests
- **Response body**: Retry-After information

