### 24. CORS - THEORETICAL CONCEPTS

**Purpose**: Control which origins can access resources.

**Same-Origin Policy**:
- Security feature of browsers
- Block requests from different origins
- Protects against CSRF attacks
- Applies to JavaScript only

**Origin Definition**:
- Scheme (HTTP/HTTPS)
- Domain (example.com)
- Port (8080)
- All three must match

**CORS Headers**:

**Request Headers**:
- **Origin**: Where request comes from
- **Access-Control-Request-Method**: Expected HTTP method
- **Access-Control-Request-Headers**: Expected headers

**Response Headers**:
- **Access-Control-Allow-Origin**: Allowed origins
- **Access-Control-Allow-Methods**: Allowed methods
- **Access-Control-Allow-Headers**: Allowed headers
- **Access-Control-Allow-Credentials**: Allow credentials
- **Access-Control-Max-Age**: Preflight cache duration

**Preflight Requests**:
- Browser sends OPTIONS first
- Server responds with allowed methods/headers
- Then browser sends actual request
- For "complex" requests

**Simple vs Complex Requests**:
- **Simple**: GET, HEAD, POST with basic headers
- **Complex**: PUT, DELETE, or custom headers
- **Complex**: Need preflight

**CORS Credentials**:
- **Without credentials**: Default, no cookies/auth
- **With credentials**: Include cookies/auth
- **credentialMode**: 'include' on client
- **Access-Control-Allow-Credentials**: true on server

