#### Q17: Explain WebSockets vs REST APIs.
**Answer:**

| Aspect | REST API | WebSocket |
|--------|----------|-----------|
| **Communication** | Request-response (one-way initiation) | Bidirectional (both sides) |
| **Connection** | Stateless, new connection per request | Persistent connection |
| **Latency** | Higher (connection overhead) | Lower (single connection) |
| **Use case** | CRUD operations, general data fetch | Real-time updates, chat, gaming |
| **Complexity** | Simple | More complex |
| **Scaling** | Easy | Requires connection management |

**REST API Example:**
```javascript
// Client requests data
GET /api/users/notifications
Response: [notification1, notification2]

// Must poll repeatedly for new data
setInterval(() => {
  fetch('/api/users/notifications')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(notifications => updateUI(notifications));
}, 3000);  // Every 3 seconds

// Inefficient!
```

**WebSocket Example:**
```javascript
// Server setup
const io = require('socket.io')(server);

io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  console.log('User connected');

  // Send notification to specific user
  socket.on('get-notifications', () => {
    socket.emit('notifications', [notification1, notification2]);
  });

  // Broadcast to all users
  socket.on('post-created', (post) => {
    io.emit('new-post', post);
  });

  socket.on('disconnect', () => {
    console.log('User disconnected');
  });
});

// Client
const socket = io('http://localhost:3000');

socket.emit('get-notifications');
socket.on('notifications', (data) => {
  updateUI(data);
});

// Real-time updates
socket.on('new-post', (post) => {
  addPostToFeed(post);
});
```

